      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The value of coronary angioplasty in elderly patients with angina pectoris is disputed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "We studied the effects of coronary angioplasty in patients more than 70 years of age during a four-year follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Successful coronary angioplasty was done in 136 patients with the age 70-83 years. All patients had high-grade angina, and 58% had multivessel disease. One procedure-related death occurred. The symptomatic effect of coronary angioplasty was good and persisting during the follow-up period. Additional revascularizations were done in 23% of the patients, most of these in the first year due to restenosis. Complete revascularization was done in 54%. After four years, 88% of the patients were alive."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "INTERPRETATION",
          "content": "In selected elderly patients coronary angioplasty can be performed safely and provides good symptomatic relief and favourable long-term outcome. There is need for additional revascularizations especially during the first year, mainly because of restenosis."
        }
      ]},
